WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.432
Long documents first, the question last.

2
00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:15.533
When a prompt includes long material (Anthropic’s threshold is roughly 20,000 tokens and up), put the documents near the top and the question, instructions, and output format at the end.

3
00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:26.139
Anthropic reports that queries placed at the end improved response quality by up to 30 percent in its tests, especially with complex multi-document inputs.

4
00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:37.853
Google’s Gemini guide gives the same advice and adds a small touch worth copying: after a large block of data, bridge to your question with a phrase like “Based on the information above.

5
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:38.431
”.

6
00:00:38.711 --> 00:00:41.207
This connects to the week 1 lesson on context.

7
00:00:41.487 --> 00:00:51.130
Research such as “Lost in the Middle” found that models used information at the start and end of long inputs more reliably than material buried in the middle.

8
00:00:51.410 --> 00:01:01.991
You can’t control where the relevant sentence sits inside a 60-page contract, but you can control where your instructions sit: at the end, right before the model starts writing.

9
00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:05.471
Two more habits from Anthropic’s long-context guidance.

10
00:01:05.751 --> 00:01:16.247
Wrap each document in its own tag with its metadata, for example a <document> containing a <source> and a <document_content>, so the model can cite which file a fact came from.

11
00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:25.103
And for question answering over long documents, ask the model to first quote the passages that bear on the question, then answer from those quotes.

12
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:31.698
Quotes give you something to verify: you can check with a string search that each quote actually appears in the source.
